  "parts": [
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q06_a",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": ["property_identification"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall that current in a filament causes heating, so consider how filament temperature changes when current decreases and how resistance of a metal depends on temperature due to lattice ion vibration and electron collisions."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: Temperature of filament decreases as heating effect is reduced, so resistance decreases."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Resistance of a metal filament depends on temperature. Current produces a heating effect with power $P = I^2 R$, so a smaller current gives less heating and the filament temperature falls. In a metal, higher temperature means greater amplitude of vibration of lattice ions and more frequent collisions with conduction electrons, giving higher resistance. Therefore when temperature falls, collision rate falls and resistance decreases."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Stating resistance increases because lower current is assumed to mean higher resistance from $R = V/I$ without considering that $V$ also changes and that filament is non-ohmic.",
        "Omitting the temperature link and only stating resistance decreases, which does not earn the explanation mark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q06_b_i",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": ["direct_calculation"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall the definition of resistance linking potential difference $V$ and current, and rearrange to make current the subject in terms of $V$ and $R$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "A1: current = V / R from $R = V/I$."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Resistance is defined by $$R = \\frac{V}{I}$$ where $V$ is potential difference across the wire and $I$ is current. Rearranging to make current the subject: $$I = \\frac{V}{R}$$ This is the current in terms of $V$ and $R$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Inverting the relation to give $IR/V$ or $VR$, confusing subject of $R = V/I$.",
        "Introducing $N$, $e$ or $L$ unnecessarily in this part, although only $V$ and $R$ are needed."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q06_b_ii",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "question_patterns": ["algebraic_derivation"],
      "hints": [
        "Start from the drift speed relation $I = nAve$ where $n$ is number density of free electrons, $A$ is cross-sectional area and $v$ is drift speed, and identify that $n$ must be found from total number $N$ divided by volume $AL$.",
        "Substitute $n = N/(AL)$ into $v = I/(nAe)$ and then substitute $I = V/R$ from (b)(i), then cancel $A$ to leave $v$ in terms of $V$, $L$, $R$, $N$ and $e$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "C1: Use $I = nAve$ with $n = N/(AL)$ where $AL$ is volume.",
        "A1: $v = I/(nAe) = (V/R)/[(N/AL)e] = VL/(RNe)$."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Current is related to drift speed by $$I = nAv e$$ where $n$ is number of free electrons per unit volume, $A$ is cross-sectional area, $v$ is average drift speed. Rearranged: $$v = \\frac{I}{nAe}$$ Number density $n$ is total number divided by volume. Volume of cylindrical wire is area times length: $$\\text{volume} = AL$$ so $$n = \\frac{N}{AL}$$ Substitute $n$ and $I = V/R$ from (b)(i): $$v = \\frac{V/R}{\\left(N/AL\\right)Ae}$$ The area $A$ cancels: $$v = \\frac{V/R}{Ne/L}$$ $$v = \\frac{VL}{RNe}$$ with units of $\\text{m s}^{-1}$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Using $N$ directly as number density $n$, forgetting to divide by volume $AL$, leading to missing factor $L$.",
        "Failing to cancel cross-sectional area $A$ correctly or introducing unknown $A$ into final expression when question requires only $e, L, N, R, V$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q06_b_iii",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": ["algebraic_derivation"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall that average time to travel length $L$ at constant drift speed $v$ is $t = L/v$, or equivalently total charge $Ne$ divided by current $I$, then substitute $v$ from (b)(ii) or $I$ from (b)(i)."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "A1: $t = L/v = L/(VL/RNe) = RNe/V$, or $t = Q/I = Ne/(V/R) = RNe/V$."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Time for uniform motion is distance divided by speed: $$t = \\frac{L}{v}$$ Substitute $v = VL/(RNe)$ from (b)(ii): $$t = \\frac{L}{VL/(RNe)}$$ Invert denominator: $$t = \\frac{L RNe}{VL}$$ Cancel $L$: $$t = \\frac{RNe}{V}$$ Equivalently, total charge passing is $Q = Ne$ and $t = Q/I = Ne/(V/R)$ gives the same result, with units of $\\text{s}$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Inverting $t = L/v$ to $v/L$, giving $V/(RNe)$ instead of $RNe/V$.",
        "Confusing total charge $Ne$ with elementary charge $e$ alone when using $t = Q/I$."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
